course: religion-ethics
lesson: 34
title: Virtues: honesty, fairness and kindness
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Aesop's fable a shepherd boy cried "Wolf!" for fun so many times that, when a real wolf came, nobody believed him; trust is built on honesty, and once it is broken it is hard to rebuild.
explain: Honesty means telling the truth and not deceiving; a white lie, like saying you love a present you dislike, is told to be kind, and people disagree about such lies, but there is often a kind way to be honest: "Thank you, it was so thoughtful of you."
explain: Fairness means no favourites; it does not always mean everyone gets the same: equality gives everyone the same, while equity gives each person what they need to have the same chance, like glasses for the child who cannot see the board.
explain: Kindness is choosing to help, include and care, and it grows from empathy, imagining how another person feels, "walking in their shoes"; Christians remember Jesus' Golden Rule: do to others what you would want them to do to you.
explain: Bullying, hurting again and again someone who finds it hard to defend themselves, is always wrong, and bystanders can help stop it; gratitude, noticing good things and saying thank you, makes people happier and kinder.
fact: Honesty means telling the truth and not deceiving others.
fact: Trust is built on honesty; once broken, it is hard to rebuild.
fact: In the fable by Aesop, an ancient Greek teller of animal stories, the boy who cried wolf was not believed when a real wolf came.
fact: A white lie is a small lie told to be polite or to spare feelings; people disagree about whether white lies are right.
fact: There is often a way to be both honest and kind, for example thanking someone for their thought.
fact: Fairness means treating people without favourites, equally or according to what they need or deserve.
fact: Equality gives everyone the same; equity gives each person what they need to have the same chance.
fact: Kindness is choosing to help, include and care for others.
fact: Empathy means imagining how someone else feels, "walking in their shoes".
fact: Jesus taught the Golden Rule: do to others what you would want them to do to you.
fact: Bullying is hurting someone again and again who finds it hard to defend themselves; it is always wrong.
fact: Bystanders can help stop bullying by not joining in, supporting the victim and telling an adult.
fact: Gratitude means noticing good things and saying thank you; grateful people tend to be happier and kinder.
exercise: What does honesty mean? | Telling the truth and not deceiving others | Saying whatever makes people like you | Keeping quiet so you never get in trouble | Telling the truth only to your friends, never to anyone else
exercise: Why did nobody come when the real wolf attacked? | The boy had lied so often that no one believed him | The villagers were all fast asleep in their beds that night | The wolf was too quiet for anyone to hear | The villagers did not care about the sheep
exercise: What is the lesson of the boy who cried wolf? | Liars lose trust even when they tell the truth | Wolves are far more dangerous than most people think | Shepherds should never be left to work on their own | Shouting loudly enough always brings help very quickly
exercise: What is a white lie? | A small lie told to be polite or spare feelings | A lie told on purpose to get someone else into trouble | A lie that turns out to be true later, by pure luck | A lie that is written down on paper instead of spoken
exercise: Do all people agree that white lies are right? | No, people disagree about them | Yes, everyone says they are fine | Yes, the Bible commands them | No, everyone says they are the worst lies
exercise: Gran knitted you a scratchy jumper. Which reply is honest and kind? | Thank you, Gran, it's lovely that you made it for me | I love it, it's the best jumper in the whole wide world, honest | It's scratchy and I'm never wearing it | Why didn't you just buy me a game
exercise: What does fairness mean? | Treating people without favourites | Giving the most to your best friend | Letting the oldest always choose first | Doing whatever the teacher likes best
exercise: What is the difference between equality and equity? | Equality gives the same; equity gives what each needs | Equality is for adults; equity is for children | Equality is fair; equity is always unfair | There is no difference at all between them, they mean the same
exercise: A girl who cannot see the board gets glasses and the others don't. Is that fair? | Yes, she gets what she needs to have the same chance | No, everyone must get glasses too, or nobody should have them | No, it is favouritism by the teacher | Yes, because her parents asked loudly
exercise: What is kindness? | Choosing to help, include and care for others | Doing what you are told so you get a reward later | Being nice only to people who are nice back to you | Giving away the things you no longer want or need
exercise: What is empathy? | Imagining how someone else feels | Feeling sorry only for yourself | Agreeing with everyone you meet | Forgetting what others have said
exercise: What is the Golden Rule that Jesus taught? | Do to others what you would want them to do to you | Do to others what they have already done to you first | Do to others whatever your parents tell you | Do good only to people who can pay you back
exercise: What makes something bullying? | Hurting someone again and again who finds it hard to defend themselves | One argument between two friends who then make up | A teacher giving a hard test to the whole class | A game where both teams sometimes lose
exercise: What can a bystander do about bullying? | Refuse to join in, support the victim and tell an adult | Film it on a phone and share it with everyone in the class | Walk away and forget about it straight away | Laugh so the bully does not pick on you
exercise: What is gratitude? | Noticing good things and saying thank you | Asking for more and bigger presents next time | Paying back every single favour straight away | Feeling proud of all the nice things you own
exercise: Why does honesty matter so much in friendship? | Friendship is built on trust, and trust on honesty | Honest people always win more games | Friends must tell each other every single secret they have | Lying is only wrong if you get caught
test: Ben dropped Mum's phone and the screen cracked; he wants to say the cat did it. What is the best-reasoned choice? | Tell the truth; a lie might be found out and would break Mum's trust | Blame the cat, because nobody can punish a cat | Hide the phone so nobody knows anything | Say nothing, because it was an accident anyway
test: Your little sister retells the fable: "The boy cried wolf and everyone came every time, the end." Put her right. | After his lies nobody came when the real wolf did | He never cried at all and the wolf went home | The villagers chased the boy away right at the very start | The wolf turned out to be his pet dog all along
test: "just say you love the drawing, who cares, it's a lie but whatever" What is the best answer? | You can be kind and honest: praise what you really like in it | Lie, because people's feelings matter more than the truth every time | Tell her it's rubbish, because truth is all that counts | Say nothing and walk off so you don't have to choose
test: Mr Ellis gives Josh, who uses a wheelchair, the room with the ramp. "Not fair!" says a kid. Best-reasoned reply? | It is fair: Josh needs the ramp to have the same chance | It isn't fair: everyone must have exactly the same room | It is fair because Mr Ellis is the teacher, end of | Who cares, it's Josh's problem, not ours
test: "move, loser, nobody wants you on the team" — Leo says this to Sam every break. What is it? | Bullying: hurting Sam again and again | Just a joke, so it doesn't count | Fair, because Leo is better at football | A normal argument between equals
test: A Hindu friend asks: "Do Christians think only Christians should be kind?" Best answer? | No, Christians believe everyone should be kind, and many faiths teach it | Yes, kindness belongs to Christians only | Yes, other people cannot really be kind | No, Christians are told not to be kind
test: You see Mia crying alone at lunch. Which shows empathy? | Imagine how lonely she feels and ask if she wants to sit with you | Think she's just being dramatic and ignore her so she stops crying | Tell everyone she was crying | Wait for a teacher to notice instead
test: There are 9 sweets and 3 kids; Ollie, the biggest, takes 5 "cos I'm bigger". Is his reason fair? | No, being bigger does not give a fair claim to more | Yes, bigger people always deserve more because they need more food | Yes, because he got there first | No, but only because sweets are unhealthy
test: Classmate: "Saying thank you is pointless, they had to give it to you anyway." Best-reasoned reply? | Thanks shows you noticed their kindness, and it makes both of you happier | Thanks is pointless, you're right, nobody needs it | You only say thanks so you get more next time | Saying thanks is a rule, so just do it
test: Judge's follow-up: "Is it ever right to stay silent when a friend is bullied?" | No; staying silent can let it go on, so tell an adult and support them | Yes, because it isn't your fight | Yes, because the friend might be the bully later | No, because you should hit the bully back
test: Why might people stop believing someone who exaggerates all the time? | They learn not to trust what that person says | Because exaggerating is against the law in most places | Because people never believe anything children say | Because they are jealous of other people's good stories
test: crap, I told Dad I finished my homework but I didn't. what now? | Own up to Dad and do the homework now | Keep quiet and hope he never checks your books | Copy a friend's homework fast before he finds out | Say the teacher forgot to set any homework this week
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the moral of Aesop's fable of the boy who cried wolf? | Habitual deceit forfeits trust, such that even truthful words are disbelieved | Wolves constitute a greater danger than villagers commonly suppose | Solitary labour is inadvisable for persons of tender years | Loud exclamation invariably secures prompt assistance
exam: Identify the principal distinction between equality and equity. | The former confers identical provision; the latter, provision according to need | The former applies to adults; the latter solely to minors | The former is just; the latter invariably unjust | The two terms are wholly synonymous
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established definition of bullying. | Repeated harm inflicted upon a person who has difficulty defending himself or herself | A single disagreement between parties of equal standing | Any instance in which a pupil is corrected by a teacher | Competition in which one team is defeated by another
exam: Ascertain the formulation of the Golden Rule as taught by Jesus. | Do unto others as you would have them do unto you | Do unto others as they have done unto you | Do unto others as your elders instruct | Do unto others solely as they deserve, according to their merit
